      "completion_tokens": 1160,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, which means it is composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means that the electrons in the water molecule are not shared equally between the oxygen and the hydrogen atoms. The oxygen atom has a slight negative charge, while the hydrogen atoms have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, a liquid, and a gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When water freezes, the molecules lock into a rigid crystal lattice.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we are most familiar with. It is the only common natural substance that exists in all three states.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When water is heated, it turns into a gas that expands and fills the space around it.\n\n### 3. The \"Universal Solvent\"\nBecause of its polarity, water is excellent at dissolving other substances. It is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This property is crucial for biological processes, allowing nutrients to move through cells and waste to be removed.\n\n### 4. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that make life possible:\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth (oceans) and the human body.\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots against gravity.\n\n### 5. Essential for Life\nBiologically, water is the medium of life. It makes up about 60% of the human body and is involved in almost every chemical reaction that keeps us alive, including digestion, temperature regulation, and waste removal."
